    "summary": "Any Hawaii employer with one or more employees — full-time, part-time, permanent, or temporary — must provide workers' compensation coverage, with limited statutory exclusions. Employers typically purchase coverage from authorized insurance carriers, and employees cannot be required to contribute to premiums. Alternatively, employers may self-insure by proving financial solvency and receiving director approval. The law was enacted in 1915 and prohibits employees from filing civil action against employers for work-related injuries.",
