    "title": "South Dakota At-Will Termination Rules and Exceptions",
    "summary": "South Dakota employment relationships may be terminated at will, meaning employers do not need a specific reason to fire an employee. Exceptions include when a contract exists, when termination is in retaliation for refusing to commit an unlawful act, or when an employee is exercising a lawful right such as filing a workers' compensation claim. State law also prohibits discharge based on race, color, creed, religion, sex, ancestry, disability, or national origin, or for serving as a juror. Employers may not fire employees solely for using tobacco products off the worksite during non-working hours.",
